<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common dispute on chickpea shipments is a count-per-ounce mismatch on kabuli lots — count is measured by weighing a fixed sample and counting seeds, and small sampling or moisture differences between buyer and seller labs can shift the reported count by a grade or two. State the sampling method in your purchase order (not just a target count number), and ask what method our certificate of analysis uses so both sides are reading the same test. This is the pulses-industry equivalent of the viscosity-testing dispute we see on guar gum — a methodology gap, not usually bad faith.</p>
<p>The second common issue is moisture and admixture (foreign matter, damaged or discoloured seed) exceeding contract tolerance on arrival rather than at loading — chickpeas are hygroscopic like most pulses, and inadequate container ventilation or moisture ingress during transit can push a compliant shipment over tolerance by the time it's inspected at destination. We pack and stow with this in mind, and would recommend asking any supplier directly how they manage in-transit moisture risk rather than assuming it's automatic.</p>
<p>Third, fumigation timing matters more than buyers expect — chickpeas are a stored product and most destination markets require a fumigation certificate against insect infestation, but fumigation needs a minimum exposure period before loading, so a rushed order timeline can genuinely collide with getting this step done properly. Build fumigation lead time into your order schedule rather than assuming it's a same-day formality.</p>
<p>What first-time UAE buyers most often get wrong: ordering "chickpeas" without specifying kabuli or desi, or specifying kabuli without a count grade. Both omissions produce a quote that isn't directly comparable to anyone else's, and re-quoting after the fact costs everyone time. Specify type and grade upfront and the rest of the process moves considerably faster.</p>
